TEXT 160 -64 Left 2 ;See voltage at petersen coil
TEXT 248 -32 Left 2 !va a 0 pwl 0s 450A 1s 450A
TEXT 280 -40 Left 2 ;controlled Petersen
TEXT 312 -16 Left 2 ;Petersen voltage risis slow without wavering\nindicating good compensation\nVoltage has maximal value at best\ncompensation
TEXT -120 200 Left 2 ;small asymetric capacitance\nin phase 1 for petersen coil
TEXT 64 -208 Left 2 ;See that line to earth \nvoltages differs if \npetersen voltage risis
TEXT -416 104 Left 2 ;From 0s on:\nThe Network delivers capacitive reactive power to the voltage source\nQ0=3x-10MVAr = -30MVAr ( capacitive power due to the line capacitances)\n \n \n \nStarting with 1s:\nThe line has a earth fault in phase 3 at 1s. \nThe voltages of phase 1,2 goes from 66.6kV to 115.5kV RMS\nThe voltage at the petersen goes from 1kV RMS to 66kV RMS\nThe petersen current goes from 8A to 472 A\nThe reactive power of the petersen goes to + 30MVAr\nThe capacitive reactive power of the network goes to 2xQ0=-60MVAr\nThis is -30MVAr in phase 1,2 and zero in phase 3\nExplanation: The remaining two phases have now capacitances at 110kV \ninstead of 65kV. If all three phases would have 110kV Q0 would go with 3 times\nthe value before 1s. Since one phase has 0Volt Q0 is 2 times the value before 1s. \nSince now the petersen QPet is +30MVAr one part of the additional Q0 of the lines\nis compensated so again the Qsum is -30MVAr and so for the voltage source\nthe Q0 is the same as before 1s\nNOTE: The voltages  L1-L2 , L2-L3, L3-L1 remains unchanged during earth fault\nThe voltages L1-N and L2-N has a phase angle of 60degrees instead of\n120 degrees before fault (see picture below)
TEXT -40 -232 Left 2 ;Voltage RMS L1-N , L2-N, L3-N
TEXT -40 584 Left 2 ;Voltage RMS L1-L2 , L2-L3, L3-L1
